            question: 'Define Price Elasticity of Demand (PED) and explain the formula.',
            answer: 'Price Elasticity of Demand (PED) measures the responsiveness of quantity demanded to a change in price. It indicates how sensitive consumers are to price changes.\n\nFormula: PED = (% change in quantity demanded) / (% change in price)\n\nAlternatively: PED = [(Q2 - Q1) / Q1 × 100] / [(P2 - P1) / P1 × 100]\n\nKey Points:\n1. PED is always negative due to the inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded (law of demand)\n2. We use absolute values for interpretation: |PED|\n3. The formula calculates percentage changes to allow comparison across different products and markets\n\nExample Calculation:\nIf chicken rice price increases from $3.50 to $4.00 (14.3% increase) and quantity demanded falls from 100 plates to 85 plates per day (15% decrease):\nPED = -15% / 14.3% = -1.05\n|PED| = 1.05 (demand is elastic)\n\nThis means a 1% increase in price leads to a 1.05% decrease in quantity demanded. Understanding PED helps businesses make pricing decisions and governments predict the impact of taxes or subsidies.',
