            question: 'Explain how to interpret different PED values: elastic, inelastic, unitary, perfectly elastic, and perfectly inelastic.',
            answer: 'PED values indicate the degree of responsiveness of quantity demanded to price changes:\n\n1. Perfectly Inelastic (|PED| = 0)\n- Quantity demanded does not change when price changes\n- Vertical demand curve\n- Example: Life-saving medication like insulin for diabetics\n\n2. Inelastic (0 < |PED| < 1)\n- Quantity demanded changes by a smaller percentage than price\n- % change in Qd < % change in P\n- Example: Rice in Singapore (necessity) - 10% price increase might only reduce consumption by 3%\n\n3. Unitary Elastic (|PED| = 1)\n- Quantity demanded changes by exactly the same percentage as price\n- % change in Qd = % change in P\n- Example: Mid-range electronics where 5% price cut leads to exactly 5% higher sales\n\n4. Elastic (|PED| > 1)\n- Quantity demanded changes by a larger percentage than price\n- % change in Qd > % change in P\n- Example: Luxury handbags - 10% price increase might reduce demand by 25%\n\n5. Perfectly Elastic (|PED| = ∞)\n- Consumers buy any quantity at one price, but nothing at higher prices\n- Horizontal demand curve\n- Example: Agricultural products in perfectly competitive markets\n\nImportance: Understanding these classifications helps firms optimize pricing strategies and helps government predict policy outcomes.',
