            question: 'Explain how availability of substitutes affects PED with Singapore examples.',
            answer: 'The availability of substitutes is one of the most important determinants of PED. The more substitutes available, the more elastic the demand.\n\nWhy Substitutes Matter:\nWhen price increases, consumers can easily switch to alternatives if many substitutes exist. This makes demand highly responsive to price changes.\n\nElastic Demand (Many Substitutes):\n\n1. Soft Drinks: Coca-Cola vs Pepsi vs F&N vs Store Brands\n- If Coca-Cola raises prices by 15%, consumers easily switch to Pepsi\n- Estimated |PED| > 2 (highly elastic)\n- Small brand loyalty, many perfect substitutes\n\n2. Kopitiam Food: Chicken Rice vs Nasi Lemak vs Wonton Mee\n- If chicken rice stall increases price from $3.50 to $4.50, customers switch to nearby alternatives\n- Many substitutes within walking distance\n- Demand is elastic\n\n3. Ride-Hailing: Grab vs Gojek vs ComfortDelGro Taxis\n- 20% surge pricing causes many to switch platforms or use traditional taxis\n- High substitutability makes demand elastic\n\nInelastic Demand (Few/No Substitutes):\n\n1. Insulin for Diabetics\n- No substitutes for life-saving medication\n- |PED| close to 0 (perfectly inelastic)\n- Patients must purchase regardless of price\n\n2. Singapore MRT (in certain areas)\n- Limited alternatives for residents without cars\n- Buses might be available but slower\n- Relatively inelastic despite fare increases\n\n3. Specialist Medical Services\n- If only one pediatric cardiologist in Singapore, parents have no alternatives\n- Demand highly inelastic\n\nConclusion: The substitute determinant explains why generic products have elastic demand while unique or specialized products have inelastic demand.',
