            question: 'Explain how proportion of income affects PED with Singapore examples.',
            answer: 'The proportion of income spent on a good significantly affects PED. Goods taking up a large proportion of income have elastic demand, while goods taking up a small proportion have inelastic demand.\n\nWhy Proportion Matters:\nConsumers notice and respond more to price changes of expensive items because these significantly impact their budget. Small-value items barely register even with large percentage price changes.\n\nElastic Demand (Large Proportion of Income):\n\n1. HDB Flats (Largest Purchase for Most Singaporeans)\n- Average 4-room flat costs $400,000-$500,000\n- Represents 5-8 years of median household income\n- Even 5% price increase ($20,000-$25,000) significantly impacts affordability\n- Buyers extremely price-sensitive, consider alternatives like smaller flats or different locations\n- Demand is elastic\n\n2. Certificate of Entitlement (COE) for Cars\n- Cat A COE: $80,000-$100,000 (2023-2024)\n- Represents 2-3 years of median salary\n- 10% increase ($8,000-$10,000) causes many to delay purchase or buy motorcycles instead\n- Highly elastic demand\n\n3. Overseas University Education\n- UK/US university: $50,000-$80,000 per year\n- 20% increase might push families to choose local universities (NUS/NTU)\n- Very elastic demand\n\nInelastic Demand (Small Proportion of Income):\n\n1. Table Salt\n- Costs $1-$2 per kg, lasts months\n- Even 100% price increase ($1 to $2) barely noticeable in monthly budget\n- Demand perfectly inelastic - consumers buy regardless of price\n\n2. Tissue Packets\n- Costs $0.50-$1.00\n- Even doubling price has negligible budget impact\n- Highly inelastic demand\n\n3. Public Transport Single Trip\n- MRT fare $1.00-$2.50\n- 10% increase ($0.10-$0.25) too small to change behavior significantly\n- Relatively inelastic\n\n4. Newspaper ($1-$1.50 daily)\n- Price increase from $1 to $1.20 unlikely to reduce purchases significantly\n- Inelastic demand\n\nConclusion: This determinant explains why luxury goods and major purchases have elastic demand while daily necessities have inelastic demand, influencing both business pricing strategies and government tax policies.',
