            question: 'Explain how luxury vs necessity classification affects PED with Singapore examples.',
            answer: 'The classification of goods as necessities or luxuries significantly affects PED. Necessities have inelastic demand while luxuries have elastic demand because consumers can postpone or forgo luxuries but cannot easily reduce consumption of necessities.\n\nNecessities (Inelastic Demand):\n\n1. Rice and Basic Food Staples\n- Essential for daily survival\n- Even 20% price increase from $2/kg to $2.40/kg barely reduces consumption\n- Singaporeans might switch brands but maintain quantity consumed\n- Estimated |PED| = 0.3-0.5 (inelastic)\n- Cannot substitute with non-food items\n\n2. Basic Healthcare Services\n- GP consultation: $30-$50\n- When sick, patients must visit doctor regardless of price\n- 15% increase unlikely to deter necessary medical visits\n- Highly inelastic: |PED| = 0.2-0.4\n- Health is non-negotiable necessity\n\n3. Utilities (Water, Electricity)\n- Essential for modern living\n- 10% electricity tariff increase reduces consumption by only 2-3%\n- Cannot significantly reduce air-conditioning in tropical Singapore\n- Inelastic demand: |PED| = 0.3-0.6\n\n4. School Textbooks\n- Required for education\n- Parents purchase regardless of price\n- Very inelastic: |PED| close to 0.1\n\nLuxuries (Elastic Demand):\n\n1. Designer Handbags (Gucci, Prada, LV)\n- Not essential, purely status symbols\n- Price increase from $2,000 to $2,500 (25%) significantly reduces purchases\n- Consumers postpone buying or switch to mid-range brands\n- Highly elastic: |PED| = 2.0-3.0\n\n2. Fine Dining Restaurants\n- Can substitute with hawker centers or home cooking\n- 20% increase in average bill from $150 to $180 causes many to dine elsewhere\n- Elastic demand: |PED| = 1.5-2.0\n- Economic downturns severely impact reservations\n\n3. Luxury Cars (Mercedes, BMW)\n- Can substitute with Japanese cars or public transport\n- $150,000 to $180,000 increase causes buyers to reconsider\n- Elastic: |PED| = 1.5-2.5\n\n4. Overseas Holidays\n- Can postpone indefinitely\n- 30% increase in Europe package tours causes switch to regional destinations (Malaysia, Thailand)\n- Very elastic: |PED| = 2.0-3.0\n\n5. Entertainment Subscriptions (Netflix, Spotify)\n- Easily cancelled if prices rise\n- Singapore saw cancellations when Netflix increased from $13.98 to $16.98\n- Elastic demand: |PED| = 1.2-1.8\n\nGrey Area - Context Dependent:\n\nSmart Phones:\n- Basic phone: Necessity (inelastic)\n- Latest iPhone Pro: Luxury (elastic)\n- Same product category, different PED based on model\n\nConclusion: This determinant is subjective and income-dependent. What is a luxury for low-income households may be a necessity for high-income households. However, generally, goods that fulfill basic needs have inelastic demand while discretionary purchases have elastic demand.',
