            question: 'Explain how addictiveness/habit formation affects PED with Singapore examples.',
            answer: 'Addictiveness and habit formation make demand highly inelastic because consumers find it difficult or impossible to reduce consumption despite price increases. The physiological or psychological dependence overrides price considerations.\n\nHighly Addictive Products (Very Inelastic):\n\n1. Cigarettes (Most Significant Example)\n- Nicotine is highly addictive\n- Singapore cigarette prices: $14-$15 per pack (70% tax component)\n- Despite 400% price increases over 20 years, consumption fell slowly\n- Smoking rate: 18.3% (1992) to 10.1% (2022) - gradual decline despite massive price increases\n- Estimated |PED| = 0.3-0.5 (highly inelastic)\n- Smokers reduce other spending rather than quit\n- Price increases mainly deter new smokers, not existing addicts\n\n2. Alcohol (Especially for Heavy Drinkers)\n- Beer, wine, spirits all taxed heavily in Singapore\n- Tiger Beer: $10-$12 per pint at bars\n- Duty increases have minimal impact on alcoholics\n- Social drinkers more elastic, but alcoholics highly inelastic\n- |PED| for heavy drinkers = 0.2-0.4\n- Liquor licensing restrictions also limit availability\n\n3. Coffee (Mild Addiction)\n- Many Singaporeans have daily kopi/coffee habit\n- Kopi: $1.20 → $1.50 (25% increase) barely reduces consumption\n- Starbucks: $6 → $7.50 still maintains loyal customer base\n- Caffeine dependence creates withdrawal symptoms (headaches, fatigue)\n- Moderately inelastic: |PED| = 0.6-0.8\n- Habitual morning coffee non-negotiable for many\n\n4. Gambling (Behavioral Addiction)\n- Casino entry levy: $150 per day for Singapore residents\n- Marina Bay Sands, Resorts World Sentosa still attract regular gamblers\n- Problem gamblers continue despite financial losses\n- Very inelastic for addicts: |PED| close to 0.1-0.3\n- Government uses levy as deterrent, not revenue tool\n\nHabit Formation (Moderately Inelastic):\n\n1. Bubble Tea Trend\n- Young Singaporeans developed daily bubble tea habits (2018-2023)\n- Typical price: $4-$8\n- Even 20% increases maintain most regular customers\n- Moderately inelastic: |PED| = 0.7-0.9\n- Habitual consumption but not physiologically addictive\n- Eventually, some reduce frequency\n\n2. Fast Food (McDonald\'s, KFC)\n- Convenience habits formed over years\n- Regular customers continue despite gradual price increases\n- McSpicy meal: $6 → $7.50 over 5 years\n- Slightly elastic for habitual consumers: |PED| = 0.8-1.0\n\nPolicy Implications:\n\nSin Taxes Effectiveness:\n- Work better at deterring new users than stopping addicts\n- Generate stable tax revenue due to inelastic demand\n- Singapore uses high cigarette taxes (70% of price) knowing consumption won\'t fall proportionally\n\nRevenue vs Health Objectives:\n- Inelastic demand ensures tax revenue\n- But also means price alone insufficient to reduce consumption significantly\n- Requires complementary policies: smoking bans, graphic warnings, age restrictions\n\nConclusion: Addictiveness and habit formation create the most inelastic demand curves. This explains why governments heavily tax these products - stable revenue despite price increases. However, the inelasticity also means taxation alone cannot solve public health problems, requiring multi-pronged approaches combining price and non-price measures.',
