            question: 'Explain the relationship between PED and total revenue when demand is elastic.',
            answer: 'When demand is elastic (|PED| > 1), there is an inverse relationship between price and total revenue. This means price changes and total revenue move in opposite directions.\n\nThe Relationship:\n- Price increases → Total Revenue decreases\n- Price decreases → Total Revenue increases\n\nWhy This Happens:\nWhen demand is elastic, consumers are highly responsive to price changes. The percentage change in quantity demanded is greater than the percentage change in price.\n\nFor elastic demand:\n% change in Qd > % change in P\n\nTherefore, the quantity effect dominates the price effect:\n- When P↑: The loss in quantity sold is proportionally larger than the price gain\n- When P↓: The gain in quantity sold is proportionally larger than the price loss\n\nMathematical Relationship:\nTotal Revenue (TR) = Price (P) × Quantity (Q)\n\nExample 1: Electronics During IT Fair\n\nScenario: Laptop sales at Challenger/Courts\n\nNormal Period (Higher Price):\n- Price: $1,500 per laptop\n- Quantity: 100 units sold monthly\n- Total Revenue = $1,500 × 100 = $150,000\n\nIT Fair Period (Price Decrease):\n- Price reduced to: $1,200 (20% decrease)\n- Elastic demand: |PED| = 2.0\n- Expected quantity change: 2.0 × 20% = 40% increase\n- New quantity: 100 + 40 = 140 units\n- New Total Revenue = $1,200 × 140 = $168,000\n\nAnalysis:\n- Price decreased 20% ($1,500 → $1,200)\n- Quantity increased 40% (100 → 140)\n- Total Revenue increased by $18,000 (12% increase)\n- The quantity gain (+40 units × $1,200 = $48,000) exceeded the price loss on original units (100 × -$300 = -$30,000)\n\nExample 2: Luxury Restaurant Pricing\n\nScenario: High-end restaurant at Marina Bay\n\nPrice Increase Strategy (Failed):\nBefore increase:\n- Average bill: $200 per customer\n- Customers: 500 per month\n- Total Revenue = $200 × 500 = $100,000\n\nAfter 20% price increase:\n- New price: $240\n- Elastic demand (luxury): |PED| = 1.5\n- Expected quantity change: 1.5 × 20% = 30% decrease\n- New customers: 500 - 150 = 350 per month\n- New Total Revenue = $240 × 350 = $84,000\n\nAnalysis:\n- Revenue fell from $100,000 to $84,000 (16% decrease)\n- Price increase backfired due to elastic demand\n- Lost customers ($20,000 revenue loss from 150 customers) exceeded gains from higher prices on remaining customers\n\nExample 3: Air Ticket Flash Sales\n\nNormal Period:\n- Singapore to Bangkok: $400\n- Passengers: 1,000 monthly\n- TR = $400,000\n\nFlash Sale (50% discount):\n- Price: $200\n- Elastic demand for leisure travel: |PED| = 2.5\n- Quantity change: 2.5 × 50% = 125% increase\n- New passengers: 1,000 + 1,250 = 2,250\n- New TR = $200 × 2,250 = $450,000\n\nAnalysis:\n- Despite halving prices, revenue increased by $50,000\n- Massive increase in passengers (125%) more than compensated for lower fares\n- Airlines use this strategy during off-peak periods\n\nBusiness Implications:\n\n1. Penetration Pricing: When launching new products with elastic demand, start with low prices to maximize revenue by capturing high quantity\n\n2. Promotional Sales: Deep discounts work for elastic goods (electronics, fashion) because revenue increases despite lower unit prices\n\n3. Avoid Price Increases: For products with elastic demand, raising prices is dangerous - will lose revenue\n\n4. Volume-Based Strategy: Focus on selling more units at lower prices rather than higher margins on fewer units\n\n5. Market Expansion: Lower prices can open new market segments, dramatically increasing total market size\n\nGraphical Representation:\n- When demand is elastic, the demand curve is relatively flat\n- A price decrease causes movement along the flat demand curve, leading to proportionally larger quantity increase\n- The area under the curve (representing TR) expands when price falls\n\nConclusion: For businesses selling products with elastic demand (luxuries, goods with many substitutes), the key to maximizing revenue is through competitive pricing and high volumes, not premium pricing. This explains why electronics retailers, fashion brands, and airlines frequently run promotions - they know lower prices will increase their total revenue.',
