            question: 'Explain tax incidence when demand is elastic, with diagram description.',
            answer: 'Tax incidence refers to who ultimately bears the burden of a tax - consumers or producers. When demand is elastic, producers bear a larger share of the tax burden than consumers because consumers are highly responsive to price changes and will drastically reduce purchases if prices rise significantly.\n\nWhy Elastic Demand Shifts Tax Burden to Producers:\n\nWhen demand is elastic (|PED| > 1):\n- Consumers are very price-sensitive\n- Many substitutes available\n- Easy to reduce consumption or switch products\n- If producers try to pass tax burden to consumers through higher prices, quantity demanded falls dramatically\n- Producers forced to absorb most of the tax to maintain sales volume\n- Consumer price rises only slightly, producer net price falls substantially\n\nDiagram Description:\n\nInitial Equilibrium (Before Tax):\n- Original supply curve: S₀\n- Demand curve: D (relatively flat, indicating elastic demand)\n- Equilibrium: Point E₀\n- Initial price: P₀ = $10\n- Initial quantity: Q₀ = 1,000 units\n\nAfter Tax Imposed (e.g., $3 per unit tax):\n- Supply curve shifts upward by full tax amount: S₀ → S₁\n- Vertical distance between S₀ and S₁ = $3 (full tax)\n- New equilibrium: Point E₁\n- New quantity: Q₁ = 700 units (significant fall due to elastic demand)\n\nPrice Changes:\n- Consumer price rises to: Pc = $10.50 (small increase of $0.50)\n- Producer receives: Pp = $7.50 (after paying $3 tax from $10.50)\n- Producer price falls by: $2.50\n\nTax Incidence Distribution:\n- Total tax per unit: $3.00\n- Consumer burden: $10.50 - $10.00 = $0.50 (17% of tax)\n- Producer burden: $10.00 - $7.50 = $2.50 (83% of tax)\n- Ratio: Consumers pay 1/6, producers pay 5/6\n\nKey Diagram Features:\n1. Relatively flat demand curve (elastic)\n2. Small vertical distance from P₀ to Pc (small consumer price increase)\n3. Large vertical distance from Pc to Pp (large producer loss)\n4. Significant horizontal contraction from Q₀ to Q₁ (elastic response)\n5. Consumer burden (shaded area) is narrow but short\n6. Producer burden (shaded area) is wider and tall\n\nExample 1: Luxury Handbags in Singapore\n\nScenario: Government imposes 10% luxury goods tax\n\nBefore Tax:\n- Designer handbag price: $2,000\n- Quantity sold: 1,000 bags monthly\n- Demand is elastic: |PED| = 2.0\n- Many substitutes: other luxury brands, mid-range alternatives\n\nAfter 10% Tax ($200 per bag):\n\nIf producer tried to pass full tax to consumer:\n- New price would be: $2,200\n- 10% price increase × PED of 2.0 = 20% quantity decrease\n- New quantity: 800 bags\n- Producer loses 200 sales\n\nActual Market Outcome:\n- Producer absorbs most of tax to maintain competitiveness\n- Consumer price rises to only: $2,050 (2.5% increase)\n- Producer receives only: $1,850 after tax ($2,050 - $200)\n- Quantity falls to: 950 bags (smaller decline than if full tax passed on)\n\nTax Burden Distribution:\n- Consumer pays: $50 extra per bag (25% of tax)\n- Producer absorbs: $150 loss per bag (75% of tax)\n- Total tax revenue: $200 × 950 = $190,000\n- Consumer contribution: $50 × 950 = $47,500\n- Producer contribution: $150 × 950 = $142,500\n\nWhy this occurs:\n- Many substitutes (Gucci vs Prada vs Coach)\n- Consumers easily switch brands or delay purchases\n- Luxury classification makes demand elastic\n- Producers cannot raise prices significantly without losing market share\n\nExample 2: Restaurant Meals\n\nBefore GST Increase:\n- Average meal price: $20\n- Meals served: 10,000 monthly\n- Elastic demand: |PED| = 1.5 (many dining alternatives)\n\nAfter 2% GST Increase (from 8% to 10%):\n- Additional tax: $0.40 per meal\n\nMarket Adjustment:\n- Restaurants raise prices to: $20.15 (absorb 62.5% of tax)\n- Consumer pays: $0.15 extra\n- Restaurant absorbs: $0.25\n- Quantity falls to: 9,775 meals\n\nTax Incidence:\n- Consumer burden: 37.5%\n- Producer burden: 62.5%\n- Elastic demand forces restaurants to absorb most tax\n\nExample 3: Electronics\n\nLaptop market:\n- Original price: $1,000\n- $100 tax imposed\n- High elasticity: |PED| = 2.5 (many brands, models, alternatives)\n\nOutcome:\n- Consumer price: $1,020\n- Producer receives: $920\n- Consumer pays: 20% of tax\n- Producer absorbs: 80% of tax\n\nPolicy Implications:\n\n1. Taxing Luxury Goods:\n- Singapore taxes luxury cars, yachts, expensive watches\n- Elastic demand means producers/importers bear most burden\n- Tax achieves revenue without significantly burdening consumers\n- May discourage imports and sales\n\n2. International Competitiveness:\n- If Singapore taxes elastic goods heavily, producers may relocate\n- Example: High-end retail might shift to neighboring countries\n- Balance needed between tax revenue and business environment\n\n3. Deadweight Loss:\n- Elastic demand causes larger quantity decrease\n- More trades prevented by tax\n- Higher economic inefficiency\n- Larger deadweight loss triangle in diagram\n\n4. Revenue Considerations:\n- Despite high tax rate, revenue may be limited\n- Elastic response reduces quantity traded\n- Total tax revenue = tax per unit × quantity sold\n- Quantity falls significantly when demand is elastic\n\nConclusion:\nWhen demand is elastic, attempting to tax such goods heavily results in producers bearing most of the burden. Consumers simply reduce purchases or switch to alternatives. This makes luxury goods taxes politically popular (wealthy bear burden) but economically inefficient (large deadweight loss). The diagram clearly shows the disproportionate burden on producers through the large gap between consumer price and producer net price, contrasted with the small increase in consumer price.',
